    {"ip": "10.108.57.10", "inferred_role": "vpn_auth_target_internal_app", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:11:02+0000", "last_seen": "2026-03-20T01:11:02+0000"},
    {"ip_range": "10.108.57.1-20", "inferred_role": "internal_subnet_swept_post_vpn_auth", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:13:18+0000", "last_seen": "2026-03-20T01:21:40+0000"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.5", "inferred_role": "out_of_scope_host_smb_probed", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:22:05+0000", "last_seen": "2026-03-20T01:26:14+0000"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.15", "inferred_role": "out_of_scope_host_smb_probed", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:22:05+0000", "last_seen": "2026-03-20T01:26:14+0000"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access — Trusted Relationship", "technique": "T1199", "what_happened": "At 2026-03-20 01:11:02 UTC, a VPN authentication request hit the internal app endpoint 10.108.57.10 carrying data.srcip=10.108.57.250 — the third-party vendor's admin gateway appliance sitting in the corporate DMZ. The session presented a vendor service account and a vendor-gateway client identifier. The vendor holds a CONTRACTED, AUTHORIZED VPN trust used for scheduled maintenance of a small set of application servers. The authentication itself is within the trust — but it occurred at 01:11 UTC, outside any documented maintenance window, and was immediately followed by activity that falls OUTSIDE the contracted scope. Wazuh rule 92902 'Lateral movement from third-party VPN session' (T1199) fired.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92902 correlated a VPN-auth event whose data.srcip (10.108.57.250) belongs to the vendor gateway range — NOT the employee VPN pool — with subsequent internal connection attempts originating from the same session. The rule mitre.id is T1199 (Trusted Relationship). The trust is real; the question Phase 3 must answer is whether the vendor session stayed within its contracted blast radius.", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:11:02+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery — Network Service Discovery", "technique": "T1046", "what_happened": "Beginning 01:13:18 UTC (~2 minutes after the VPN auth), the same third-party source 10.108.57.250 generated a burst of internal connection attempts sweeping the 10.108.57.1-20 range on ports 22, 445 and 3389 — a classic post-tunnel internal subnet port scan. The probe rate was deliberate (~one host every few seconds with short sleeps) consistent with bash /dev/tcp scripted probing rather than an interactive admin checking a single server. Suricata logged the sweep and Wazuh rule 31100 'Internal port scan post-VPN auth' (T1046) fired. The scanned range INCLUDES hosts the vendor is NOT contracted to touch.", "chain_reason": "Suricata recorded data.srcip=10.108.57.250 hitting many distinct data.dstip values in 10.108.57.0/24 on data.dstport in {22,445,3389} within an 8-minute window. Wazuh rule 31100 (T1046) correlated the scan to the same VPN session opened in step 1 — the source IP is identical, the timing is contiguous. A contracted maintenance session does not enumerate a /24.", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:13:18+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Lateral Movement — Remote Services (SMB)", "technique": "T1021.002", "what_happened": "From 01:22:05 UTC, after the sweep identified responsive hosts, the same source 10.108.57.250 initiated SMB connection attempts (null-session / IPC$ probes on port 445) against the discovered hosts 10.108.57.5 and 10.108.57.15. These hosts are OUT OF SCOPE for the vendor's maintenance contract — the vendor is contracted to reach a different, named set of application servers. SMB probing of out-of-scope hosts from a third-party VPN session is the lateral-movement signature this playbook (Trusted Relationship, T1199) is designed to catch: a partner connection moving BEYOND the systems it is authorized to access.", "chain_reason": "Suricata recorded data.srcip=10.108.57.250 → data.dstip in {10.108.57.5, 10.108.57.15} on data.dstport=445 immediately after the step-2 sweep, same VPN session. The destinations are NOT in the vendor's contracted host set. The progression VPN-auth → /24 sweep → SMB on out-of-scope hosts is a single contiguous chain from one third-party source — the canonical Trusted Relationship abuse pattern.", "first_seen": "2026-03-20T01:22:05+0000"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-03-20T01:11:02+0000", "end": "2026-03-20T01:26:14+0000"},
  "alert_count": 2,
  "unique_steps": 3
}
